const REACTIONS = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Reactions — the authoritative physical world state for one duel. You own src/Confluence.Reactions/** and tests/Confluence.Reactions.Tests/**.
Read spec §4 (lines 130-180) in full, the 'Arena materials and generation catalogue' chapter (654-688), §3 status/statuses paragraphs (108-121), 'Numerical interpretation' (523-530), every biome environment_rule (node tools/cat.mjs list biomes --fields id,name,environment_rule), and the world_interaction text of the Heat and Water kits (node tools/cat.mjs kit WIZ-002; kit WIZ-010; kit WIZ-024) plus a sample of others (grep world_interaction for steam, ice, wind, wet, electric, dust, growth, venom, prism, shadow) so the API supports how spells actually deposit and consume material.
Build:
1. CellGrid: 0.5 m interaction cells over the arena square (origin + dims derived from ArenaLayout diameter), each storing Water, Heat, Frost, Dust, Sand, Growth units 0..4 plus MaterialFlags and timers (burn suppression, damp, charge, ice durability/expiry, venom). Initialize from ArenaLayout.Materials (natural sources flagged) and solid material flags (metal/conductive/porous/combustible…). Active region bookkeeping with the 128-region cap (spec L136): adjacent effects merge into contiguous regions; never delete meaningful state at random; document how the cap is enforced.
2. Deposits/operations queued during a tick, then ReactionWorld.Step(tick) processes them in the fixed order GlobalRules.World.ReactionOrder (spec L165): incoming direct impacts -> heat-water / heat-ice / frost-water consumption -> electricity connectivity -> wind movement -> decay. Each reaction consumes its stated inputs before another reaction reads the cell; energy can never exceed the source budget (self-sustaining burn-water-steam-condense loops run out). Footprints: point, circle, rectangle/lane, line/segment with width, cone; default strengths per spec L163 (quick projectile 1 local unit; field 1 per covered cell on creation; heavy direct projectile 2 at impact + 1 in adjacent cells within 1 m); cap 4 units per cell; flags gate reactions (heat on painted stone does not make it combustible).
3. Implement EVERY row of the core reaction table (spec L142-L161) with its exact numbers from GlobalRules.World (add missing ones locally with refs): heat+water -> steam (consume 1 heat + 1 water per overlapping cell; 3 s base lifetime; 2 m rise; no damage); frost+water -> ice tiles (2 frost consume 1 water; 5 s; 45 durability per tile); heat+ice melt (2 heat -> 1 water); force+brittle ice shard cone (>=20 direct damage impact breaks struck tile, 1 m cone, 10 physical, once per impact); electricity + connected wet cells (0.6 s charge, <=6 m propagation and <=12 cells, first contact deals the source's declared reaction damage); electricity + metal (one conductive object, <=3 branches, 6 m each, glow before release); water + anchored conductor/grounded prop (redirect one branch, overload after capacity); wind + steam/dust (translate up to 3 m/s; dispersal of the front 2 m slice per GlobalRules thresholds — read D-023 in DECISIONS); water + burning material (remove 1 heat, suppress burn 2 s); heat + combustible growth (>=2 heat ignites, burns 2 s, declared damage, consumes growth); water + sand (damp sand 3 s, reduces dust production, no immobilization); force + loose props (emit an impulse request for Sim — props live in Sim; respect mass/velocity caps from GlobalRules.Terrain); sound + solid cover attenuation query; light + prism reflection query (one reflection, 70% retained damage); shadow + bright light (concealment volume lifetime -1 s per s of exposure to LightShafts); venom + water dilution (50%, original bounded connected patch); gravity field projectile deflection query (max 20 degrees, physical projectiles only); arcane dispel (consume one removable field layer, never permanent geometry).
4. Volumes: server-owned obscuration volumes (steam, ash, mist, dust, shadow concealment, venom cloud, illumination…) with owner, kind, lifetime, opacity rules, ability to be condensed/consumed. Owner bookkeeping: emit VolumeCreated/VolumeRetired events carrying owner so Sim can count player-created clouds against the 3-field cap (spec L128, L670) and request retirement of the oldest. SightPathLength(from, to) = cumulative path length through sight-blocking volumes; BlocksAcquisition(from, to) when >= 2 m (spec L167; GlobalRules). Steam never intercepts projectiles, never disables audio, never wets everyone inside by default.
5. Interaction with bodies: Step takes the current bodies (IReadOnlyList<ReactionBody>: id, position, radius, height, isGrounded) so it can report ContactDamage requests (body id, amount, DamageChannel, owner, source event id, reaction kind, isSecondary=true) for electrified patches, burning growth, shard cones, red-hot surfaces, venom clouds; it never applies damage itself. Queries for Sim: IsWetAt, SurfaceAt(pos) -> surface kind (normal, wet stone, ice/slippery, damp sand, sticky growth, burning, charged…), CellUnits(pos), active volumes enumeration, conductor registration API (RegisterConductor/Grounding for props and solids with ids, segment or box, grounded flag, capacity), biome environment hooks (scheduled refill pulses like Raincourt's 8 s rain into gutter beds — implement a generic scheduled-source mechanism plus BIO-001's exact rule; others can follow later).
6. Snapshot/StateHash: compact serializable state of active regions and volumes for replication/replay, and a deterministic hash.
Tests (this is a required test area of the project): every reaction row incl. its caps/budgets and consumption; fixed-order determinism (same deposits in different queue orders within one tick produce the documented result); steam path-length sight blocking (1.9 m does not block, 2.0 m does); wind translation speed cap and dispersal; electricity propagation limits (6 m, 12 cells, disconnected pools do not conduct); ice durability and melt; no energy creation over many ticks (source-strength accounting); region cap behavior; Fireball-into-Water-Pool scenario (Heat 2 impact at a Water 2 pool edge makes a bounded steam patch); Bubble Buckler heat interaction is Sim-level, skip.
Mark [ContentBehavior] on nothing except BIO-001 if its rain refill rule is completely implemented here (it still needs Sim wiring, so prefer NOT marking it; report it instead).`

const CASTING = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Casting — cast phases, cancellation/interrupt/refund/recast rules, mastery normalization, and the per-wizard casting grammar framework. You own src/Confluence.Casting/** and tests/Confluence.Casting.Tests/**.
Read spec §3 'Common numerical foundation' through 'Casting phases and interruption' (76-97), 'Global modifier and trigger rules' (122-129), the whole 'Wizards, casting, and player mastery' chapter (460-517), §9 Accessibility (363-374), §14 cast grammar validation (425), and the wizard records' casting_mode/input_sequence/normal_cast/mastery for ALL 36 wizards (node tools/cat.mjs list wizards --fields id,name,casting_mode,input_sequence,normal_cast,mastery). Classify the 36 grammars into families and design a framework that can express every one of them (button timing, held gauges, multi-key sequences, gesture/rune strokes with normalization and direction-key equivalents, voice syllables with an equivalent rhythm route, combat-event-triggered grammars like parry/hit-confirm, movement/velocity/distance grammars, widget/dial/tile puzzles, scheduling/memory). Implement fully: WIZ-002 Bram Cinder (pressure release) and WIZ-010 Halen Tide (pressure metering). Also a trivial 'guided normal' grammar used by tests/bots.
Design (names are suggestions; keep them coherent):
- CastInputFrame per tick: tick, held/pressed/released button bits (Primary, Guard, Sprint, Jump, Crouch, Dodge, Melee, Signature, ActiveItem, Consumable, Recast, CastModifier, ModifierA, ModifierB), aim yaw/pitch, caster position/velocity/grounded, and optional discrete GrammarEvents (syllable id with local timestamp, gesture stroke samples, external combat events like 'perfect guard', 'melee hit', 'dodge completed'). Define an InputButtons [Flags] enum here; Sim and Net will reuse it.
- CastableSpell: the per-spell timing/cost data the machine needs (id, mana, cooldown ticks, intrinsic windup ticks from cast_time, recovery class/ticks with defaults 0.20 quick projectiles / 0.35 heavy+control / 0.45 major fields+summons (spec L92), channel max (<=3 s), movement multiplier while casting (0.70 heavy/control/field, 0.85 quick; stationary override), interrupt-refund tag, minimum anticipatory cue 0.25 s for >70 direct damage, hard control or teleport destination (spec L96; part of preparation, never a secret delay), damaging vs nondamaging (for mastery reward choice), grammar-specific parameters).
- ICastHost (implemented by Sim): current mana, TryPay(mana) honoring cost floor and refund caps, cooldown remaining per spell, IsSilenced/IsHardControlled/IsWarded (spawn alcove), CanCastWhileSprinting, current tick.
- CastMachine (one per player): Idle -> Preparation (grammar-driven; selected spell shown; exposure tracked) -> Commitment (mana paid, cooldown starts) -> Windup (intrinsic cast_time) -> Release (spawns effect; may Channel up to 3 s, cancellable any tick, remaining ticks stop) -> Recovery -> Idle. Rules: cancellation before commitment spends no mana, preserves elapsed exposed preparation, and applies a 0.15 s re-cast lock; interrupting a committed cast spends its resource and starts the normal cooldown, 25% mana refund only with the universal interrupt-refund tag; voluntary cancel after commitment refunds nothing; no refund > 25% of paid or more than once per cast (spec L90). Recast: pending recast state per selected spell with window/count from the spell; Recast acts only on the selected spell's pending state, never starts a new spell, reuses the original cast event identity and mastery result, costs nothing unless stated (spec L94). Spell selection change mid-preparation cancels (no swap windup). Emit typed events (PreparationStarted, CueStarted, Committed{castEventId, mastery}, Released, ChannelTick, ChannelEnded, RecoveryEnded, Cancelled{reason}, Interrupted{refund}, Recast{castEventId}) for Sim, UI and replays. Cast event ids are deterministic per player.
- IGrammar: Begin(spell, context) / Advance(frame) returning Continue | Commit(MasteryResult) | Cancel(reason) | AutoCommit, plus a GrammarUiState for the client HUD (gauge value, windows, band, hints) and an exact miss reason when mastery fails ('late release', 'left band', 'too fast toggling'... never just 'bad cast', spec L500). Grammars run identically on server (authoritative, from received input frames) and client (prediction/UI). Grammar input validation: accepted input intervals (e.g. Halen 0.1 s), minimum preparation, release order, rate limits; inputs faster than accepted intervals give no extra benefit (spec L478).
- MasteryResult and MasteryClamp: exactly one reward per cast (efficiency <= 20% combined with equipment/augment efficiency on the same rewarded cast; damage, projectile speed, windup each <= 15% shared category limit, spec L478-L480, L124); recasts/splits/delayed portions inherit the source cast's single result.
- GrammarRegistry keyed by wizard id; a test asserts all 36 wizard ids have a registered grammar family descriptor even if only WIZ-002/WIZ-010 have executable grammars (unimplemented ones must throw NotImplemented clearly, never silently cast).
WIZ-002 (Bram): hold primary 0.20-0.60 s; visible gauge rises and falls; release after 0.20 s casts normally; overholding to 0.60 s auto-vents a normal cast; no misfire/self-damage; mastery: release within the marked 100 ms crest window at 0.45 s -> +12% direct damage, or 12% less mana on nondamaging spells; one reward per spell. Decide and document what happens on a release before 0.20 s (follow the roster's analogous rules, e.g. WIZ-001 queues a normal cast at 0.20 s) and the exact window bounds.
WIZ-010 (Halen): hold primary to compress a pressure gauge; Modifier A vents it; release primary between 0.30 and 0.90 s to cast; any release inside the safe 20-80 band casts normally; leaving the band automatically vents and offers a normal cast after 0.30 s (mana only on that cast); mastery: keep pressure within 45-55 continuously for 0.25 s before release -> 15% less mana; toggling faster than 0.1 s accepted interval adds no benefit. Choose and document compress/vent rates so mastery is achievable but demanding.
Tests: phase timing exactness in ticks, cancel/interrupt/refund caps, re-cast lock, recast rules, channel cancellation, cue minimums, both grammars' normal/mastery/miss-reason paths across edge timings, the auto-vent paths, input-rate validation (spam does not help), determinism. Tag the grammar tests [Verifies("WIZ-002")] / [Verifies("WIZ-010")] only for grammar aspects (the wizard record also needs signature/passive/spells later, so do not mark [ContentBehavior] on the wizards).`

const ARENA = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Arena — seeded, server-authoritative procedural arena generation producing Confluence.Core.Arena.ArenaLayout. You own src/Confluence.Arena/** and tests/Confluence.Arena.Tests/**.
Read spec §7 (302-335) in full, §4 'Terrain and impact rules' (169-176), the 'Arena materials and generation catalogue' chapter (654-688), §14 replay/seed notes, and the arena catalogue: all 24 biomes, 72 modules, 48 props (node tools/cat.mjs list biomes / modules / props with --fields; the md also has 'Arena biomes' at 39570, 'Terrain modules' 39959, 'Interactive props' 41404 for prose).
Build:
1. Module geometry library: for each of the 72 modules, an authored geometry builder producing Solids/ramps/material patches/sockets in module-local space from its footprint, height_m, geometry text, materials and durability text (e.g. MOD-001 Split Gate: two 2.8 m doors divided by an anchored center pillar; MOD-025 Short Ramp: gentle ramp to a 1 m deck with two return routes; MOD-062 Four Pillar Center: four thin pillars around an 8 m clear final floor). Ramps <= 30 degrees, stairs standardized, route width >= 2.5 m, durability values from the text (anchored = 0/indestructible; soft cover in the final center retires at 85 s). Aim for all 72; implement at least every module needed so the generator can assemble arenas for all four diameters (each category: Topology, Cover, Elevation, Material, Approach, Center). Mark a module [ContentBehavior("MOD-xxx")] when its authored geometry is complete and used by the generator, and add a [Verifies] test per module (footprint, height, sockets/route width, durability values, flags).
2. Biome dressing: each biome contributes its materials and its announced environment-rule geometry (e.g. BIO-001 Raincourt: two mirrored 2 m gutter beds as natural water sources with the refill rule string; BIO-002 two root beds with Heat 1 Growth 1; BIO-004 frozen water tiles; BIO-006 four 3 m rail sections; BIO-003 two anchored prisms...). The gameplay rule itself runs in Reactions/Sim later; you place and flag its sources/props symmetrically. Do not mark biomes [ContentBehavior] (their rules are not executable yet); report what geometry each needs.
3. Props placement: 3-8 prop definitions per arena, normally two instances each (mirrored or resource-equivalent pairs), <=48 instances, never the only route, spawn floor, or final-circle support (spec L662, prop 'placement' text). Accept an IContentAvailability filter for props and biomes so the server can restrict to implemented ones.
4. Generator pipeline exactly per spec L314-L323: pick size/biome/environment modifier/seed within the schedule constraints (use Confluence.Core.Rules.Schedules diameter sequence helpers; diameters 24/32/40/48; balanced per four rounds; usable area within 10% of target), assemble a mirrored route skeleton (two spawn alcoves, central final-duel floor, two side approaches, one longer firing lane, >= two crossover routes) from modules with matching sockets/heights, add cover modules and props by biome material/visibility budget, cosmetic variation that never changes collision, place protected spawns, central boundary destination, standardized lighting, then VALIDATE: reachability, route redundancy (>= 2 disjoint approaches), initial shortest spawn-to-spawn path 16-34 m by size, no direct spawn sightline, central cover reachable in 2.0-3.5 s by a mobile melee build (define precisely; document), at least two approaches interrupt the firing lane, longest uninterrupted firing lane 12-28 m, cover 18-30% of usable floor, no single cover blocks every approach, elevation 0-3 m (4 m vantage with two access routes and a safe descent), min route width 2.5 m (clearance-aware navigation), mirrored distances/resources/cover/elevation parity per spawn, contraction route validity (every accessible floor cell keeps a route to the final floor under the boundary contour over time, spec L676), and simulated destruction states and representative field placements (removing destructibles never opens holes or seals regions; a field/wall at any single chokepoint leaves an alternative route). Retry up to 8 seeds, then use a prevalidated fallback layout for that size and biome; never release an invalid arena. Boundary: irregular validated closed contour (BoundaryContour InitialRadii) contracting to the 4 m-radius central floor.
5. Public navigation API (ArenaNav: walkable grid with heights + clearance, shortest paths, line-of-sight queries) reusable by bots and validators.
6. Determinism: same (matchSeed, round, request) -> byte-identical layout (assert via a layout hash). Arena RNG stream only (RngStreams Arena). Independent of player builds (spec L325).
Tests (required test area: procedural arena fairness): large seed batches for each size and biome (e.g. 24 biomes x 4 sizes x >= 10 seeds, plus a 500-seed batch at each size) all valid or fallback with reported fallback rate; mirrored distances equal within tolerance; no spawn sightline; route width; cover ratio; firing lane bounds; path lengths; contraction validity; fallback layouts valid; determinism; diameter schedule balance; prop parity and caps. Keep the full test project under ~2 minutes (use [Trait] to separate a longer batch if needed).`

const ECONOMY = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Economy — loadouts, equipment shop, eligibility predicates, structural spell profiles, dormancy, augment drafts, feat ledger. You own src/Confluence.Economy/** and tests/Confluence.Economy.Tests/**.
Read spec §5 (181-289) in full, §6 (290-301), 'Exclusive spell rules and transformation profiles' (519-553), 'Shared equipment rules' (554-591), 'Free augments and eligibility specification' (592-653), 'Worked builds and conditional progression' (689-717), §8 'Shop and codex' (351-355), §14 security (429), plus representative catalogue records (equipment of each category/activation, augments of every predicate leaf type, transformations with exclusive groups, builds BLD-037..048).
Build:
1. Loadout model: wizard, owned kit (8 spells) and six equipped spells (default first six; distinct; only own kit), equipment slots (Main Focus accepts wand/staff/weapon; Sidearm wand or weapon; a definition cannot occupy both; Armor, Clothing, Boots, Trinket A/B (two different legal trinkets), Consumable) with issued plain items (no bonus, no resale, outside the 120 catalogue) when empty, three reserve slots, nonempty Unique group cannot be equipped twice, exactly <=1 enabled equipment active (others dormant, labeled) and <=1 consumable enabled, consumable charges (one per purchase; unused persist; used sells for 0), paid price tracking for 50% resale, six augment slots with rank 1-2.
2. EquipmentShop per player per shop phase: three offer positions each with one independent free reroll; offers drawn per DECISIONS D-020 from unlocked (Schedules tier unlock by mode/round) affordable tiers among legal, AVAILABLE (IContentAvailability), non-visible definitions; a rerolled card cannot return the definition it replaced or any current visible one; no rolled item costs more than the current balance; after a purchase the slot is empty until next shop; remaining unaffordable cards fold into an outside-budget placeholder (no free redraw) whose unused reroll can draw from the new affordable pool; empty pool -> Save Crowns without consuming the reroll; selling refunds 50% (consumable used -> 0 and disappears) and never refills cards or restores rerolls. All operations are atomic transactions with request ids (duplicate request ids are rejected/idempotent), validated money/slots/unique groups, and return explicit result codes. Uses the Offer RNG stream (RngStreams) only.
3. PredicateEvaluator implementing EVERY leaf (IPredicateVisitor) over an EligibilityContext: wizard, loadout, structural profiles of the six equipped spells, owned kit, active (non-dormant) augments with ranks/families/tags, scheduled round number (use Schedules.PredicateRound for Duel per DECISIONS D-029), and completed-duel feat counts. Exact semantics per the predicate grammar table (spec 604-637): spell_tag counts equipped structural profiles (also: tags on the same qualifying spell); spell leaf = owned kit; equipped_tag counts distinct equipped definitions supplying a live tag (a disabled active does not supply active-effect tags like active_equipment; buying two copies does not create two sources); owned_item includes reserve; equipped_category with distinct_unique_groups; inclusive thresholds; explicit id lists restrict counting; family counts count distinct families. Plain-language explanation of each failing leaf for card UI (reuse PredicateDescriber).
4. Structural profile (two-profile rule, spec 243, 543): base kit tags + active wizard transformations' StructuralDeltas (Core registry) + chosen equipment structural changes; general shared augment effects excluded. Acquisition-only leaves (wizard, round_at_least, feat) are checked at acquisition and their acquisition-time truth value is recorded and reused afterwards (so NOT(feat) etc. cannot flip later); equipment/augment/spell leaves are ongoing.
5. DormancyResolver: after every finalized equipment/spell change and at duel start, evaluate in dependency order (topological walk over augment->augment references); a dormant augment keeps slot and rank but supplies no effect/tags/parent qualification; a dormant parent makes dependents dormant; restoring requirements reactivates at the next finalization; report missing requirements per augment. Cycles are invalid content (validator already checks).
6. AugmentDraft per player per draft round: rarity from Schedules; freeze loadout; five distinct eligible cards; at opening include >= 1 current-wizard spell transformation when one is eligible, >= 2 universal or broadly conditional shared alternatives, the remaining 2 sampled from the full eligible pool; owned rank-1 definitions can appear as rank-2 offers at any draft whose rarity >= original (Schedules.AllowsRankTwoOffer); owned rank 2 never appears; exclusive-group conflicts with owned active transformations are ineligible; each card has one free reroll replacing only that card, excluding every definition shown previously in this draft and all current cards; selecting one card ends the draft (pass/timeout picks nothing; nothing banked); new definition fills a free slot or REPLACES a chosen owned augment (confirmation lists dependents that become dormant; replacement gives no money/rerolls/choices); rank 2 upgrades in place. Floor: report count of legal AVAILABLE alternatives per rarity; >= 10 required (spec L233) — expose a floor check API the server calls at match start; with ContentAvailability.All the real catalogue must satisfy it for every wizard at default loadouts in every rarity (test it). Draft RNG stream only.
7. FeatLedger: per player count of completed duels meeting each FEAT (Core Feats), fed by duel results (Sim will report achieved feat ids per duel); training feats never count.
8. Worked builds: deferred item from Stage 0 — test that each BLD record's listed loadout is legal (slots, unique groups, exclusive groups) and that BLD-037..048 augments are eligible under their own stated loadout/feats per their draft plan (using ContentAvailability.All). Report genuine catalogue inconsistencies rather than weakening the evaluator.
Tests (required test areas: purchases, augment eligibility/rerolls/dormancy, content ownership): purchase legality incl. duplicate/concurrent request ids, money, tiers, slots, unique groups, sell values, consumables; shop reroll exclusions, placeholders, Save Crowns; every predicate leaf with real catalogue augments; acquisition vs ongoing; two-profile rule (a shared augment cannot qualify itself); dormancy chains and restoration; draft guarantees, distinctness, reroll exclusion across the whole draft, rank 2 rules, replacement, pass/timeout; spells never purchasable and only own-kit spells equippable (content ownership); floors for all 36 wizards x 3 rarities.`

const MATCH = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Match — match structure and round flow for Conclave and Duel. You own src/Confluence.Match/** and tests/Confluence.Match.Tests/**. The Economy and Sim libraries are being written concurrently by other agents and are EMPTY in your worktree; do not reference their types. Instead define narrow seam interfaces in Confluence.Match that the server will later implement with adapters:
- IDuelRunner: start a duel for a pairing with a DuelSetup (match seed, round, arena request, side assignment, player ids, isReplay, fullResourceRestore) and poll/tick for a DuelOutcome (winner or draw, end reason (death, surrender, time, simultaneous lethal), health fractions excluding shields/borrowed health, completed feat ids per player, timestamps).
- IMatchEconomy: OpenShop(player, income, round) / CloseShop / OpenDraft(player, rarity) / CloseDraft / FinalizeLoadout(player) / ReadyState queries — Match owns phase timing and calls these at boundaries; it never computes prices or offers itself.
- IArenaProvider: request the round arena (match seed, round, diameter) -> arena id + public preview metadata (diameter, biome, major terrain, props). All pairs in a round share the same generated arena (spec L34).
Read spec §2 (30-73) in full, §5 currency/schedule tables (191-233), §11 competitive systems (385-395, for placement/rating inputs), §14 reconnect/migration notes, and Confluence.Core.Rules.Schedules/GlobalRules.Round/Match/Economy.
Build:
1. ConclaveMatch (2-8 players for custom; 8 standard): tick-driven state machine with explicit phases: Lobby -> per round [Reveal (opponent, arena preview, Resolve, opponent's completed previous-round loadout) -> Shop 35 s (income 600 first shop, 500 later incl. bracket shops; +100 winner bonus for winning previous combat round; none for draws/byes/replays) -> Draft 30 s on draft rounds only (Schedules.DraftRarity) -> Alcove 5 s -> Combat (<= 90 s, via IDuelRunner) -> Results 8 s] -> next round / bracket / end. Early ready advances only when every ACTIVE player is ready (spec L45). Resolve: start 8; loss removes ResolveLoss(round); draws remove from both; elimination after the simultaneous round finishes; same-round eliminations tie with mean placement. Byes per spec L61 (fewest previous byes, then longest since bye, then seeded tie; preserves Resolve, baseline income, no combat win, no bonus; bye players may shop). Regular schedule ends early when one contender remains. After round 12: seeded single-elimination bracket (seeds: Resolve, combat wins, fewer byes, lobby seed), byes to reach a power of two for highest seeds, max 3 bracket rounds, every bracket loss eliminates; bracket draw replays once with same loadouts, full resources, no shop, new balanced arena seed; second draw -> shared finish at the mean of tied positions (no sole champion). Tier 4 stays unlocked in bracket; no drafts in bracket or replays.
2. Pairing (spec L59): evaluate the COMPLETE set of pairings (not greedy) minimizing cost: immediate rematch is the largest cost, repeating any of the previous two opponents next, then Resolve difference, then combat-win difference; exact ties via the recorded round seed (RngStreams Pairing). Spawn side alternates when possible; no player gets the same side more than twice consecutively when a legal alternative exists.
3. DuelMatch (spec L65): best of seven with the same round economy; tiers unlock rounds 1, 2, 4, 6 and drafts Silver 1-2, Gold 3-4, Prismatic 5-6 (Schedules); a tied round replays with same loadouts and no extra income; after two replays it scores half a win each and advances; at 3.5-3.5 one deciding round; two drawn deciders -> recorded match draw; private rematch reuse/reset wizard choices (flag only).
4. Placements and results: MatchPlacement list with tie means; per-round RoundResult (Resolve changes, income, winner bonus) for UI; a MatchEvent stream (phase changes, pairings, results, eliminations) suitable for clients and replays. Reconnect: absent players keep playing rounds normally (runner reports outcome); disconnect never pauses others (spec L55).
5. Determinism: same seed + same outcomes -> identical event stream. Custom Conclave settings object (player count 2-8, shop timer, starting Crowns, spectator delay, allowed content) with a settings fingerprint (spec L71) — store/validate settings; ranked uses fixed rules.
Tests (required test area: round transitions): full 8-player Conclave simulations with scripted fake runner outcomes covering Resolve loss schedule, eliminations, tie placements, byes with odd counts, pairing cost properties (no immediate rematch when avoidable; complete enumeration matches brute force), side alternation, bracket seeding/byes/draw replay/shared finish, early end, phase timers and early-ready, income and winner bonus amounts, draft round schedule, Duel BO7 incl. half wins and deciders, determinism.`

const SIMCORE = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Sim core — the authoritative duel simulation foundation (no spells yet). You own src/Confluence.Sim/** and tests/Confluence.Sim.Tests/**. The Confluence.Reactions and Confluence.Casting projects are being written concurrently by other agents and are EMPTY in your worktree: do NOT depend on their types. Leave clean extension seams that the next stage will connect: an ISpellRuntime hook invoked at tick-order step 3/4, an IWorldMaterials hook at step 5 (deposits, reactions, contact damage requests, sight path length, surface kind queries) with a no-op default, and a per-player casting seam. Define the InputButtons flags and PlayerInput command struct in Sim (tick, sequence, move axes, yaw, pitch, held buttons: Primary, Guard, Sprint, Jump, Crouch, Dodge, Melee, Signature, ActiveItem, Consumable, Recast, CastModifier, ModifierA, ModifierB, selected slot 0-5); the casting agent defines its own equivalent flags and the next stage will unify them, so keep yours in one small file.
Read spec §3 (74-129) in full, §4 'Terrain and impact rules' (169-176), §2 round sequence and boundary/time rules (42-56), §5 feats table (251-281), §6 fields/summons notes (290-301, 492-497), §14 multiplayer/technical (417-436) and the 'Arena layout contract' types in Core.
Build (folders suggested: World/, Physics/, Movement/, Combat/, Status/, Players/, Duel/):
1. DuelWorld: constructed from an ArenaLayout + two DuelPlayerSetup (player id, wizard id, equipped spell ids, equipment ids with categories so the main focus picks the melee profile via MeleeProfile, active augment ids/ranks — stored for later stages), Step(PlayerInput[2]) advancing exactly one tick in the ARCHITECTURE tick order; deterministic StateHash; DuelEvents per tick (DamageEvent, StatusEvent, GuardEvent, MeleeEvent, MovementEvent, FieldEvent, DuelEndEvent, …) with stable ids for replays and the results 'decisive sequence' (spec L359).
2. Collision world from ArenaLayout solids (YawBox, ramps via RampShape) plus dynamic temporary solids (walls/ice later, with durability and destruction), raycast/sphere sweep/overlap queries, sight-blocking queries.
3. CharacterMotor: a pure deterministic step function (state, input, collision, dt) usable identically by the client for prediction. Capsule character (GlobalRules.Movement dimensions/eye height/step height/acceleration from DECISIONS D-024): walk 5.5, sprint 7 (12 stamina/s, disallows channels and normal melee), crouch, jump (10 stamina), moderate air steering that cannot instantly reverse, no bunny-hop/slide stacking, dodge (25 stamina, 3 m in 0.22 s, 1.6 s cooldown, no invulnerability), ramps <= 30 degrees walkable, step-up, ground snap, slippery-surface hook (drift, not control loss), movement-bonus cap +20%, slow cap (strongest only, <= 30%), casting movement multipliers hook, knockback/displacement with the 4 m cap respecting hard-control immunity, fall damage above 3 m (10/m, cap 50), outside-map fall -> nearest valid platform + 40 health penalty + 0.5 s visible recall (no item procs), boundary-edge clamp for forced displacement (stop at edge + 5 impact damage) and teleports never ending outside.
4. Resources: 300 health, 100 mana, 100 stamina per duel (restored each duel, no passive health regen), mana regen 10/s after 1.5 s without spending (cap 14/s after modifiers; channels count as spending), stamina 20/s after 1 s; cannot spend without the full initial cost.
5. Damage pipeline exactly in spec order (L112): valid hit + guard angle -> precision multiplier (only precision-tagged, <= 1.25, no second crit) -> guard (50% frontal 120 degree arc for weapon and projectile direct damage; perfect guard first 0.12 s 80% and +5 mana once per guard; never full immunity; reduces associated knockback by the same fraction; no effect on ground fields, rear/flank, boundary, sound without physical projectile) -> channel resistance (cap 35%, floor -15%) -> other passive reductions (combined multiplicatively with resistance, total cap 45%) -> shields (temporary + reserve share a 100 cap; cast/triggered shields expire <= 8 s; reserve persist until broken; earliest-expiring absorbs first) -> health. Split-channel attacks resolve per channel and rejoin as one hit event. Damage kinds: Direct, Splash, Secondary/Proc (cannot trigger on-hit, on-kill, resource return or other procs; one generation only), Environment/Reaction, Boundary (bypasses everything, never triggers items/augments), Melee (not a spell; can trigger eligible direct-hit effects), Prop, Fall/Recall. Healing: direct healing capped 35/s per player and 120 per duel (remaining budget visible); lifesteal only from direct damage, <= 10%, <= 20/s and within the 120 budget; never from self-damage, props, summons or training targets. Max health cap 450.
6. Statuses: registry-driven named statuses (burn, venom, chill, brittle, charge, marked, wet, slow, root, stun, silence, reveal, invisibility, knockdown/displacement) with source definition ids; hard control (stun, root, silence, forced inability, hostile displacement preventing movement) single application cap 0.75 s (silence 0.6 s) and 2 s immunity afterwards during which control attempts deal damage but apply nothing and are not queued; slows strongest only, cap 30% and 3 s; DoT same-definition refresh keeps strongest intensity, different definitions coexist; dispel removes the oldest removable hostile status (or explicit selection), never boundary rules, terrain or control immunity; reveal <= 2 s (shown to the revealed); invisibility <= 1.5 s ending on attack/cast/signature/damage with a 4 m silhouette; wet = reaction eligibility only, no damage multiplier.
7. Guard & melee & dodge & equip: guard 25 stamina upfront, <= 0.8 s held, 3 s cooldown from release, clearly punishable 0.25 s release window; common melee from MeleeProfile (focus/light blade/heavy/spear) with swept strike volume matching the visible movement (no aim snap or tracking after commit), one damage per opponent per attack, prop contact, windups and recoveries, stamina costs, sprint disallows; equip/swap 0.35 s that cannot cancel recovery; selection change has no swap windup.
8. Fields and summons registry: per player <= 3 gameplay fields and <= 2 summons, each <= 8 s lifetime; creating at the limit retires the oldest eligible with a dissolution event; neutral reaction fields created by a player count against that player; ownership grants no immunity. Proc budget tracker: an effect may trigger once per 0.5 s AND once per original target-hit event (whichever is less frequent); beam ticks share; chains <= 3 jumps <= 6 m, never the same opponent twice per cast. Modifier/stat system with cap enforcement (movement +20%, CDR 25% with 0.5 s min recharge, cost floor 70%, refunds <= 25% of the payment, dash charges <= 2, dash <= 8 m, teleport <= 10 m) and raw vs effective values for the inspector (spec L355).
9. Duel flow inside the world: 5 s alcove ward (no damage or casting, movement confined to the alcove, loadout inspection), combat up to 90 s, boundary from layout.Boundary (damage 15/s first 2 s outside, 30/s, 60/s after 4 s; escalation resets after 1 s back inside; bypass all mitigation; triggers nothing), timer judgement at 90 s by health fraction (excluding shields/borrowed health; difference < 0.5 percentage points -> draw), simultaneous lethal outcomes in one step -> draw, surrender = loss. Track the 24 feats per player per duel (Core Feats with typed thresholds); some depend on spells/reactions that do not exist yet — implement the counters and the hooks they need and report which are not yet fed.
10. Lag-compensation history: ring buffer of player hit volumes for the last 150 ms (9 ticks) and a rewind query used by hitscan/melee validation; fields/projectiles/props are never rewound.
Tests: movement metrics (walk/sprint speeds, stamina drain and regen delays, jump cost, dodge distance/time/cooldown, ramps <= 30 degrees, step-up, air steering limits, fall damage, recall), resource regen caps, damage-order examples with exact numbers (guard 50/80%, resist caps and floors, 45% passive cap, shields ordering and cap, healing and lifesteal budgets), status rules (control cap, immunity, slow strongest, DoT refresh), guard arc and perfect window and release punish window, melee reach/windup/one-hit-per-opponent/sprint restriction per profile, field/summon cap retirement, proc budget, boundary escalation/reset/bypass, alcove ward, timer judgement and draw threshold, simultaneous death draw, determinism (two worlds with the same inputs produce identical StateHash every tick), lag-comp rewind. Use small hand-built ArenaLayouts in tests (the Arena generator is being written concurrently).`

const NET = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: Confluence.Net foundation — transport and protocol primitives (game-specific messages come in Stage 3 once Sim/Match exist). You own src/Confluence.Net/** and tests/Confluence.Net.Tests/**. The project already references LiteNetLib 2.1.4 (netstandard2.1 build; inspect its API in ~/.nuget/packages/litenetlib/2.1.4 — use reflection or the XML docs; do not guess method names). The other Confluence projects referenced by Confluence.Net (Reactions, Casting, Arena, Economy, Sim, Match) are EMPTY in your worktree; depend only on Confluence.Core and LiteNetLib.
Read spec §14 (417-436) and §2 reconnect rules (L55), §11 spectator delay (L391).
Build:
1. Transport wrapper over LiteNetLib: ServerTransport / ClientTransport with explicit Poll() pumping (no background threads touching game state), channels: ReliableOrdered (control, match, shop, draft, events), ReliableUnordered where useful, Sequenced/Unreliable (inputs, snapshots); connection lifecycle with a handshake carrying protocol version, client build id, ruleset hash (SHA-256 over the catalogue bytes + a rules version string; Core has no hashing, so implement RulesetFingerprint here using System.Security.Cryptography which exists in netstandard2.1 and Unity), display name, and a reconnect token; reject mismatched rulesets with a clear reason (spec L427 unsupported-version state); reconnect within 90 s resumes the same player identity.
2. Message framing and serialization: a compact binary writer/reader (or LiteNetLib NetDataWriter/Reader wrappers) with a message registry (message type id -> handler), bounds-checked reads (malformed packets are rejected and counted, never crash the server), quantization helpers for positions/angles, delta helpers.
3. Protocol primitives: clock sync and server-tick estimation (RTT/offset smoothing); InputCommandBuffer (client sends each tick's input with sequence numbers plus the last N redundant inputs; server dedupes, orders, and supplies missing ticks with the last known input, bounded); SnapshotBuffer with interpolation sampling ~100 ms behind (generic over a snapshot payload), acknowledgement of last processed input sequence for reconciliation; request/response with per-client request ids (duplicates return the original response; used for shop/draft transactions); per-message-type token-bucket rate limiting (rerolls, purchases, chat-like spam) with counters; congestion-aware snapshot rate selector between 20 and 30 Hz (DECISIONS D-005); network simulation (latency/jitter/loss) configuration for tests using LiteNetLib's built-in simulation where available.
Tests: real UDP loopback on 127.0.0.1 with ephemeral ports — handshake success and ruleset mismatch rejection, reliable ordering under simulated loss, request dedupe, rate limiting, input redundancy recovering dropped packets, snapshot interpolation correctness, clock sync convergence, reconnect token resume within the window and rejection after it (use injectable time for the window), malformed packet rejection. Keep tests fast and robust (no fixed sleeps longer than needed; poll with timeouts).`

const UNITYC = `${COMMON}
YOUR SUBSYSTEM: the Unity client foundation. You own client/** (a NEW Unity project), tools/sync-unity.ps1 and tools/build-client.ps1. Unity editor: "${UNITY}" (6000.6.4f1, licensed via Unity Hub; Windows Mono/IL2CPP player and Dedicated Server modules installed). Consult the official manual for this version when needed (https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.6/Documentation/Manual/...). PowerShell scripts must be run with: powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File <script>. The game libraries other than Confluence.Core are EMPTY in your worktree (other agents are writing them); depend only on Confluence.Core for now (sync script must still copy whichever Confluence.*.dll exist plus LiteNetLib.dll from ~/.nuget/packages/litenetlib/2.1.4/lib/netstandard2.1).
Conventions: like the user's other Unity project, build everything at runtime from code (no hand-authored scenes/prefabs beyond one bootstrap scene generated by an editor script), so the project is fully reviewable as text. Temporary procedural art is acceptable but must be listed in docs/notes/client.md as 'temporary asset' entries.
Build:
1. Create the project in client/ via batchmode (-createProject), with packages: Universal Render Pipeline, Input System, (UI Toolkit is built in), and anything else minimal you need. Configure URP from an editor script (pipeline asset + renderer, assigned in Graphics and Quality settings), Input System as the active input handling, .NET Standard 2.1 API level, Mono backend for dev builds, linear color space, company/product name 'Confluence'. Commit ProjectSettings/, Packages/manifest.json and packages-lock.json, Assets/** with .meta files; the root .gitignore already ignores Library/Temp/Logs/UserSettings/Build(s) under client/.
2. tools/sync-unity.ps1: dotnet build the netstandard2.1 libraries in Release, copy Confluence.*.dll (+ .pdb) and LiteNetLib.dll into client/Assets/Plugins/Confluence/, copy docs/confluence/confluence-catalogue.json into client/Assets/StreamingAssets/. Commit the copied DLLs? NO — add client/Assets/Plugins/Confluence/*.dll(.meta ok) and StreamingAssets catalogue to .gitignore? Decide: commit the .meta files with stable GUIDs but gitignore the binaries and the catalogue copy, and make the build script always run the sync first. Document this in docs/notes/client.md.
3. Runtime architecture (Assets/Scripts/...): a Bootstrap MonoBehaviour that creates the app shell: AppState machine (Boot -> MainMenu -> Connecting -> Lobby/Shop/Draft/Alcove/Duel/Results placeholders), CatalogueService loading the catalogue via Confluence.Core from StreamingAssets, a main menu with Play (host/port fields, name), Training, Codex, Settings (FOV 80-110, sensitivity, invert, master/music/effects/voice volume sliders), Quit — UI Toolkit built from code. An ArenaRenderer that builds GameObjects/meshes for any Confluence.Core.Arena.ArenaLayout: solids (YawBox + ramp wedges) with simple materials keyed by Solid.Material/Flags (stone, metal, wood, glass, hedge/growth, water patches, sand, ice) and readable material patterns (not color alone, spec L371), material patches as decals/quads, prop placeholders, spawn alcoves, the final floor, the boundary contour as a visible wall/ring that can be updated over time via BoundaryContour.RadiusAt, standardized overhead lighting. A first-person camera rig (eye height from GlobalRules.Movement) with a debug free-fly/walk mode for arena preview, mouse look with sensitivity/FOV settings, and a rotatable top-down arena preview camera (spec L329). Coordinate convention: Core uses Unity-style axes (+Z forward, yaw positive turns right) so mapping is direct; document any pitch sign flip (Core treats positive pitch as looking up; Unity euler X is the negative).
4. Because the Arena generator is not available in your worktree, include a small client-side SampleLayouts helper that hand-builds a plausible ArenaLayout (mirrored spawns, center floor, cover, a ramp, a water patch, boundary contour) purely for previewing; mark it clearly as a temporary debug tool to be replaced by Confluence.Arena.
5. Build automation: Assets/Editor/BuildScript.cs with BuildWindowsPlayer (development, Mono, output out/client/Confluence.exe) invoked via -executeMethod; tools/build-client.ps1 runs sync then Unity batchmode build and fails on errors (parse the log). Support command-line args in the player: -arenaPreview (load the sample layout into the walk/fly preview), -screenshot <path> and -quitAfter <seconds> for automated smoke tests.
6. Verify for real: build the player, run it with -arenaPreview -screenshot out/client-shot.png -quitAfter 8 (windowed, e.g. -screen-width 1280 -screen-height 720 -screen-fullscreen 0), then open the PNG with your Read tool and confirm the arena renders sensibly (fix and retry until it does); also run the main menu once with a screenshot. Report the exact commands and results. If Unity batchmode fails for licensing or another environmental reason, report the exact error text and continue with everything else that does not need the editor.
Tests: if practical, a Unity EditMode test assembly (Assets/Tests/Editor) that loads the catalogue through Confluence.Core and builds an ArenaRenderer for the sample layout, run via -runTests -testPlatform EditMode -testResults out/unity-editmode.xml; report results.`

phase('Implement')
const specs = [
  ['reactions', REACTIONS],
  ['casting', CASTING],
  ['arena', ARENA],
  ['economy', ECONOMY],
  ['match', MATCH],
  ['sim-core', SIMCORE],
  ['net', NET],
  ['unity-client', UNITYC],
]
// args.inPlace: { label: absoluteWorktreePath } — continue an interrupted attempt inside its existing worktree.
const IN_PLACE = (args && args.inPlace) || {}
// args.batches: [[labels], [labels]] — run batches sequentially to limit how many agents a usage-limit stop interrupts.
const BATCHES = (args && args.batches) || [specs.map(s => s[0])]
const inPlaceNote = (label) => IN_PLACE[label] ? `
CONTINUING AN INTERRUPTED ATTEMPT IN PLACE: your worktree already exists at ${IN_PLACE[label]} (NOT your shell's starting directory). Run EVERY command from there (start each Bash command with: cd "${IN_PLACE[label].replace(/\\\\/g, '/')}" && ...) and edit files only under that path. It holds your previous attempt's commits (prefixed WIP:, the last one a possibly-broken salvage of uncommitted files). Do NOT start over: read docs/notes/ in that worktree (if present), 'git log --stat master..HEAD', and the existing code; get it compiling; then finish the remaining requirements below. Be economical: re-read only the spec sections you still need. Never touch the main checkout at ${REPO}.
` : ''
const toRun = specs.filter(([label]) => !ONLY || ONLY.includes(label))
const results = []
for (const batch of BATCHES) {
  const batchSpecs = toRun.filter(([label]) => batch.includes(label))
  if (!batchSpecs.length) continue
  log(`batch: ${batchSpecs.map(s => s[0]).join(', ')}`)
  const r = await parallel(batchSpecs.map(([label, prompt]) => () =>
    agent(inPlaceNote(label) + prompt + resumeNote(label),
      IN_PLACE[label] ? { label, phase: 'Implement', schema: REPORT } : { label, phase: 'Implement', isolation: 'worktree', schema: REPORT })
      .then(x => (x ? { label, ...x } : null))))
  results.push(...r)
  if (r.some(x => !x)) { log('a batch member failed; stopping before later batches'); break }
}
const done = PRIOR.concat(results.filter(Boolean))
const failed = toRun.map(s => s[0]).filter(l => !done.find(d => d.label === l))
if (failed.length) {
  log(`FAILED: ${failed.join(', ')} — skipping merge so they can be resumed`)
  return { reports: done, failed, merge: null }
}
log(`implemented: ${done.map(d => d.label).join(', ')}; failed: ${failed.join(', ') || 'none'}`)

phase('Merge')
const merge = await agent(`${COMMON.replace('you are in an isolated git worktree of it (all paths below are relative to your worktree root, which is your current directory — run \'git rev-parse --show-toplevel\' to confirm)', 'you work directly in the MAIN checkout at ' + REPO + ' on branch master (not a worktree)')}
YOUR TASK: integrate the finished Stage 1 subsystem branches into master at ${REPO}.
Branches (label: branch @ commit):
${done.map(d => `- ${d.label}: ${d.branch} @ ${d.commit}`).join('\n')}
${failed.length ? 'These agents FAILED and have no branch: ' + failed.join(', ') : ''}
Steps:
1. git merge --no-ff each branch in this order: reactions, casting, arena, economy, match, sim-core, net, unity-client (skip missing). They own disjoint paths; resolve any conflict (likely only .gitignore or docs/notes) by keeping both sides' intent. Use 'git worktree list' / 'git branch -a' if a branch name looks wrong.
2. dotnet build Confluence.sln must give 0 errors; dotnet test Confluence.sln must pass fully. Fix integration breakage minimally and only where a cross-project problem exists (e.g. duplicate type names across assemblies that break the ledger tool or Unity's single plugin folder, namespace collisions). Do not redesign subsystems.
3. Consolidate decisions: append every decision from the reports below to docs/DECISIONS.md as new numbered entries (continue from the highest existing D-number), grouped by subsystem, concise, keeping spec line references. Do not duplicate existing entries.
4. Update docs/PROGRESS.md: Stage 1 status per subsystem, exact test counts you observed, known issues/gaps (from the reports), missing/temporary assets (from the unity-client report), and the next concrete task (Stage 1b: connect Sim to Casting and Reactions, spell runtime framework, then M1 content).
5. Regenerate the ledger: powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/ledger.ps1 (fall back to running its two steps directly if needed).
6. Commit (message ends with: Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>). Then remove the merged worktrees (git worktree remove --force <path>) and delete their branches.
REPORTS:
${JSON.stringify(done, null, 1)}
Return: merge outcome per branch, conflicts and how resolved, build/test summary with exact counts per test project, ledger summary, and remaining integration issues.`, { label: 'merge', phase: 'Merge' })
return { reports: done, failed, merge }
